 Activities report on the 1[st] year of Research fellow fellowship
During the initial two years of my PhD program, I actively engaged in multiple seminars and attended workshops and tutorials, all aimed at refining my research, writing, and technical abilities. Furthermore, within this timeframe, I successfully submitted my first journal paper and completed two abstract presentations at conferences, in addition to delivering a poster presentation at a doctoral seminar. Courses where I have participated:
   The course taught by Prof. Richard E. Ocejo titled "Studying Cities: Urban Sociology in the Twenty-First Century" offered by École des sciences politiques et sociales, Uclouvain accounted for 3 credits held at Louvain-La-Neuve. This course focused on the study of sociological theories on human dynamics in urban environments, a critical aspect for the planning of the urban experimental campaigns I will be developing in my thesis. 
   I completed a course on "The Sustainable Development Goals  -  A global, transdisciplinary vision for the future" offered by University of Copenhagen which accounted for 2 credits. In this course I learnt about the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) and strategies for implementing them in various contexts, including architecture and urban planning. I dedicated 3 weeks to study this course, and, in that time, I got to understand the United Nation (UN)'s transdisciplinary vision for a sustainable future based on 17 SDGs adopted by UN and the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC). 
   I completed a course on "Greening the Economy: Sustainable Cities" offered by Lund University which accounted for 5 credits. In this course I learnt about navigating urban transformations and how sustainable cities were built. This course required a five-week commitment on which time I enhanced my knowledge and understanding of sustainable urban development practices across various countries. The course's peer-graded assignments allowed me to apply what I learned in a practical context, contributing to my knowledge in the field of Architecture and Urban Planning for my PhD studies.
   I undertook the online training course titled "Understanding Research Methods" taught by University of London and SOAS University of London which was offered by Coursera. This training was focused on systematic review writing, which was instrumental in acquiring the necessary research skills for conducting a comprehensive systematic literature review. This training covered essential aspects such as building effective keywords, managing references, navigating databases, and extracting relevant knowledge from literature. Additionally, it provided guidance on reporting key findings in alignment with the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA) protocol, which is crucial for ensuring transparency and reproducibility in systematic reviews. The process also involved learning about project coordination, as well as ethical and legal considerations unique to systematic reviews. A significant part of the training emphasized the importance of bias assessment, critical appraisal of the literature, and the screening and eligibility assessment of studies, tasks that I undertook in collaboration with my supervisors. The systematic methodology that must be necessary for a systematic review, as detailed by Page et al. (2021) in their PRISMA guidelines, equipped me with the research skills necessary for the successful execution of the review process. This comprehensive training was crucial for synthesizing and clarifying the systematic methodology required for my thesis.
   I completed the training course "Thesis Writers' Circle" by Céline Gouverneur from the `Institut des Langues Vivantes' to improve my academic writing skills in English. All the sessions in this series were highly informative and educational which made me a better academic writer.
  
Seminars where I have participated;
   I completed a seminar organized by Prof. André Stephan and Dr. Marshal Maskarenj on Data Collection, Qualitative Data Analysis and Quantitative Data Analysis which took place at the "Institute of Land Architecture and Built Environment (LAB) research training seminars 2023"  -  4 day sessions.
   I completed a seminar conducted by Prof. André Stephan on "Forging an online presence  -  Google Scholar / Research Gate / LinkedIn / Twitter ORCID / Web of Science (Publons)" which took place at the Faculty of Architecture, Architectural Engineering, Urban Planning (LOCI) LAB Saint-Gilles on 23 February 2023.
   I also completed another seminar conducted by Prof. André Stephan on "Publish Strategically and for the greater good" which took place at the LOCI LAB Saint-Gilles on 24 Nov 2022.
   I attended a seminar on "From soil to city" organized by Brussels Municipality where Prof. Maider Llaguno was one of the speakers together with representatives from Brussels Environment, Perspective Brussels, 3D Cit-is and CityDev. These presentations focused on the formulation of sustainable urban development strategies for the Brussels Capital Region. This seminar took place on Sep 26, 2023, at perspective Brussels.
   I took part in a meeting conducted by the Institute of Psychological Sciences (IPSY) (Institut de Recherche en Sciences Psychologiques) on the 10th November 2022 on "Climate change, environmental issues, and resilience: from emotion to behaviors and vice versa" which took place at Salle du Conseil, University of Catholique Louvain (Uclouvain), Louvain-La-Neuve. The main goal of this meeting was to enable exchanges between the various researchers within IPSY working on (or planning to) climate change from a psychology science framework. In this event I was introduced to psychological measures and tools such as immersive games using Virtual Reality (VR) for detecting brain legions. I also learned various terminologies related to psychology and experienced an in-person discussion on the interactivity between psychological science and environmental behaviours.
   I attended a seminar on "Urban DMA to Walkability and Walking" by Dr Elek Pafka who was from the `Informal Urbanism Research Hub, the University of Melbourne. The seminar was organized at the LOCI LAB in Brussels on 19[th] September 2023.
   I participated in a seminar conducted by Prof. Dan Li and Prof. Maider Llaguno at LAB where I gave a briefing of my research methodology on conducting fieldwork for Outdoor Thermal Comfort (OTC) assessment.

Research dissemination: 
In my first two years of research, I have worked on the improvement of paper writing and research dissemination strategies which have led to the submission of 1 journal paper, 2 conference presentations and a conference poster. Additionally, poster and conference abstracts have been accepted:

Journal paper #1: "Environmental and psychophysiological data collection for urban outdoor thermal comfort assessment: A systematic review" authored by Pranav Pandya, Prof. Maider Llaguno-Munitxa, Prof. Martin Gareth Edwards, Dr. Emilie Lacroix, Prof. Gabriele Manoli, Prof. Ariane Middel. This will be the first journal paper of my thesis which was submitted and is currently under internal review for resubmission to Science of the Total Environment.
The systematic review concludes with the proposition of an experimental planning preparation framework. Human-centered OTC experimental planning is a complex, multi-stage process that requires field experiment planning and the definition of environmental, psychological, and physiological data collection practices. These are to be defined, and informed by the research objectives, and strategies. The experiment plan must consider target population (age, gender, sample size, sampling technique, health & sociocultural background), the chosen site (location, climate), the planned duration of the experiment (time of experiment, sampling size, study type i.e., cross-section, longitudinal and duration), and potential challenges that might be encountered (budget constraints, lack of equipment, weather). For environmental data collection, it's essential to consider the urban micrometeorology (air temperature, relative humidity, mean radiant temperature, and the wind speed and direction), other multidimensional comfort parameters that may be influencing the site (such as loud noise levels, obstructed views, excessive brightness, and glare), and urban fabric characteristics (such as sky view factor, building density, green cover). For psychological data, it is necessary to gather objective measures (such as DSB, a Stroop test, Mind Map test, or typing test), and subjective measures to better understand how individual differences in diverse populations respond within heterogeneous urban environments and evaluate the role that urban OTC has on the mental well-being of citizens. The adaptive thermal indices should be used to access participant perception to OTC, with the most use indices in literature as mentioned in framework. Regarding the physiological data, parameters related to cardiovascular, neurological, and dermatological aspects proof useful for OTC assessments. 

The systematic review highlighted various research gaps which also included:
-The investigation of psychophysiological OTC in urban settings has predominantly centered on stationary participants. Yet, in bustling cities, citizens are typically in constant motion. Therefore, delving into the realm of mobile sensing technologies, specifically through the utilization of wearable environmental and psychophysiological sensors, holds the potential to enhance our comprehension of OTC in connection to urban mobility choices and citizen preferences.
-Field studies examining the psychophysiological aspects of OTC during sports activities remain relatively scarce, particularly in light of the growing prevalence of soft mobility modes such as bicycling, skating, and running in urban areas. Given that individuals participating in physical activities through these modes may encounter distinct thermal stress, it is necessary to develop focused field studies to comprehensively evaluate their thermal experiences.

Based on this, while my initial proposal submitted to the FNRS ASP focused on general pedestrian comfort studies, following the systematic review the focus was towards studies focusing on the assessment of environmental and psychophysiological comfort of bicycle riders. This comes in a time when cities are delving into promoting sustainable development by incorporating bicycling as a transportation alternative. Therefore, it became prudent to access the OTC of bicyclists and to: i) Develop a comprehensive environmental and psychophysiological thermal comfort assessment for soft mobility users in particular to bicycle riders, and ii) Establish protocols and methodology focusing on urban environments accessible for bicycle riders -i.e. bicycle routes, streets with dedicated bicycle paths. This ongoing work has recently led to the submission of 3 abstracts focused on cycling and thermal comfort. 

Conference abstract #1: "Cycling Towards Sustainable Urban Mobility: Thermal Comfort and Well-being in Diverse Neighborhoods of Brussels" was accepted for presentation at the "Conference on interdisciplinary and transdisciplinary research for sustainable development" that took place in Louvain-la-Neuve, Belgium on 24 November 2023 as a part of CircleU. 
Abstract fragment: Urban cycling initiatives don't consider thermal comfort in the planning and design of their routes to ensure they do not inadvertently harm citizens' well-being. If certain cycling routes constitute discomforting thermophysiological characteristics that make them uncomfortable and thermal stress-inducing, then overtime with continuous exposure such conditions could undermine both the physical and psychological well-being of citizens. Therefore, it's essential that urban planning and policymaking prioritize not only the promotion of cycling as a sustainable mode of transportation but also focus on validating citizens' psychophysiological thermal comfort across the proposed routes. This work proposes a geospatial analytic approach to assess the role of thermal comfort in urban cyclability analysis, to inform urban design and cycling route planning. 

Conference abstract #2: "Modelling the Interplay between Urban Environmental Characteristics and Cyclist Route Preferences" was accepted for presentation at the "EGU General Assembly 2024" that will take place in Vienna, Austria on 14 to 19 April 2024 as part of European Geosciences Union (EGU).
Abstract fragment: In response to the escalating challenges posed by climate change on urban mobility and public health, the BCR serves as a prime example of sustainable mobility incorporated with cycling. Policy initiatives implemented in BCR such as 'Good Move' and 'Ville 30' have acted as catalysts, prompting a paradigm shift towards specialized cycling lanes and facilities, thereby enhancing the safety and convenience of cycling as a viable transportation alternative. This study focuses on a comprehensive modelling approach to investigate the impact of OTC on cycling behavior, marking a significant departure from existing methodologies. The lack of a foundational framework for conducting OTC assessments that holistically integrate psychological, physiological, and environmental sensing aspects which are particularly from a dynamic perspective such as that of a bicycle rider further highlights the novelty and importance of this research. The proposed modelling framework is aimed at establishing a replicable and scalable standard, potentially relevant across diverse urban contexts globally. This research proposes a data-driven geospatial model that includes urban design, micrometeorology, and thermal comfort indicators. Such a model is poised to offer actionable insights into optimizing urban infrastructure to foster cycling as a sustainable mode of transportation, with a keen focus on enhancing thermal comfort for cyclists. 

Poster Presentation #1: "Modelling the Interplay between Urban Environmental Characteristics and Cyclist Route Preferences" was made into poster and was accepted for presentation at the "Doctoral Seminars on Sustainability Research in the Built Environment" that will take place in Antwerp, Belgium on 30 & 31 May 2024. This poster is currently under preparation. 

I am currently working on the formulation of the geospatial thermal comfort assessment model focused on bicyclists. This model will combine a bicycle route and thermal comfort model to analyze the thermal comfort of BCR bicycling routes, and plan for healthier and inclusive bicycling routes. This ongoing work will lead to the second journal paper of my thesis. For gathering data for this paper, the following data is being processed:
 From the database of weather underground (https://www.wunderground.com/history) I retrieved a historical weather data archive which was then downloaded using tools that include web-scrapper and Application Program Interface (API) of wunderground. The retrieved data had a temporal resolution of 5 minutes for the entire BCR.
 Brussel Mobility has taken initiative to install bicycle counters which measure the number of bicycle and average speed of bicycle riders at 15 minutes intervals for 18 roads at a resolution of 15 minutes from 1st January 2019 to 31st December 2023. These measuring devices are located across BCR. From Brussel Mobility datahub, data for the number of bicycles crossing their measurement device and the average speed of riders have been collected using API call request from the link below (https://data.mobility.brussels/bike/api/counts). Metadata for each bicycle station that include unique id & location coordinates was retrieved from the link (https://data.mobility.brussels/en/info/rt_counting). These devices are shown in Figure 7 & 8. 
 From Belgium government's datahub the bicycle road network data was also retrieved with the link (https://data.gov.be/en/dataset/d4961387-23f4-44f8-b2e7-c7e9a260a83e). This data provides the designated bicycle routes for BCR.
 From UrbIS which is the cartographic and geographic information system (GIS) platform focused on the BCR, the data for green spaces (i.e. containing vegetation) was downloaded (https://datastore.brussels/web/data/dataset/espaces_verts_region_bruxelloise.xml).
 From Brussel Regional Public Service, data identifying and geolocating trees have been retrieved and mapped (http://data-mobility.irisnet.be/fr/info/trees/). 
 From project Cluster of Excellence CliSAP by University of Hamburg, Local Climate Zone (LCZ) maps were retrieved (https://figshare.com/articles/dataset/European_LCZ_map/13322450).

In parallel to this work, I am working on the preparation of an experimental campaign that will involve the preparation of an environmental and psychophysiological sensing bicycle setup designed to assess thermal comfort. This work will lead to the third journal of this research. 

The upcoming fourth journal paper will focus on analyzing video recordings captured along cycling routes. These recordings will be mapped on virtual spheres for Virtual Reality (VR) immersions that will be prepared in the Open Hub mixed reality room in Louvain-la-Neuve, hence aiming to facilitate off-site exploration. The primary objective of this study is to gather eye-tracking data to assess participants' visual attention on Urban Green Infrastructure (UGI) by examining fixations and saccades. Additionally, we aim to investigate the role of UGI in eliciting responses related to anxiety and pleasantness among participants. This research builds upon the ongoing work of Prof. Maider Llaguno and Prof. Martin Edwards within the scope of the Green Brussels project, supported by the Lourvain4City foundation.

