 Progress report on the research project
Urban GIST - Green Infrastructure for Stress reduction and Thermal comfort:

 --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Goals of the research
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Given the potential health threats that exposure to excessive heat may pose, particularly for soft mobility users (pedestrians, bicyclists, scooter riders, etc.) in face of climate change (Katzschner, 2010), providing thermally comfortable outdoor environments is crucial to promote public health. Outdoor Thermal Comfort (OTC) reflects an individual's perceived bodily feeling of being comfortable; not feeling too warm or too cold in a given urban environment (Vanos et al. 2010; Parsons 2014). The project focuses on the exploration of the role Urban Green Infrastructure (UGI) plays in citizen well-being, with a focus on OTC and thermal stress reduction.  While the positive role of nature on general well-being is widely recognized, the UGI implementation characteristics that most effectively contribute to the improvement of OTC are yet to be identified. Furthermore, previous literature has underscored that, apart from environmental Outdoor Thermal Comfort (OTC), the significant impact of psychophysiological comfort on thermal well-being cannot be overlooked, which is indeed of critical importance. However, most prior assessments of OTC, particularly those focusing on the role of Urban Green Infrastructure (UGI), have predominantly concentrated on environmental factors, neglecting the crucial role of psychophysiological comfort. To address these research gaps, this thesis proposes a novel soft-mobility centered OTC assessment methodology combining environmental, biometric sensing, and participant surveys, through the deployment of geospatial, and off-site and on-site experimental campaigns.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The thesis will address the following research questions: 
 What is the role small to medium scale UGI implementations and their characteristics -ratio, geometry, type, forest biodiversity- play in thermal comfort of soft mobility users and in particular pedestrians or bicyclists?
 --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Are environmental stressors, especially thermal stress acting as a driver of citizen distress?
  
In the first year of thesis a systematic literature review was performed, which led to the identification of research gaps which led to the formulation of the following research questions focusing on bicyclists as soft mobility users particularly susceptive to bicycle rider OTC.
  
 What are the design characteristics of urban cycling paths and their surrounding UGI associated with improved OTC for bicyclist? 
 --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Can we, through the deployment of a human-centered experimental campaign better report the multi-domain thermal comfort of bicyclists?

Hence, the following research aims to develop a research methodology using geospatial, and field experimental studies, to evaluate the urban design and UGI planning conditions that can contribute to the enhancement of physical, physiological, and psychological OTC for urban cyclists. This understanding is necessary to enhance the use of soft mobility means in cities and promote sustainable urban design and planning strategies.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	
 --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
State of the art
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The experience of thermal stress as perceived by individuals is subjective and includes environmental, physiological, and psychological comfort levels. Evaluating these comfort dimensions is crucial in assessing thermal stress, as overlooking them can lead to situations where individuals experience thermal stress more intensely than projected (Lam et al., 2021; Li et al., 2022; Mansi et al., 2021). Altering an individual's location can influence and potentially improve their levels of thermal stress, particularly when they are in close proximity to UGI (Yoshida et al., 2015).
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Prior literature that has focused on `macro' or city scale studies, have highlighted the beneficial role of Nature-based Solutions (NbS) for heat island, noise or air pollution mitigation (Johnson et al. 2020; Llaguno-Munitxa and Bou-Zeid 2020a; Manoli et al. 2019). Making use of hybrid physics and data-driven models, urban fabric, microclimate and air quality, health reports, and socio-demographic metrics have been studied, and the positive role of NbS for urban health have also been reported (Sowińska-Świerkosz et al., 2021). At the `micro' or neighborhood scale, local sensing approaches using carry-on sensing kits have enabled the study of the local environmental gradients to high spatiotemporal resolutions (Apte et al., 2017; Pigliautile & Pisello, 2018) and have reported the benefits of local UGI to lower air temperature and enhancement of thermal comfort. Publications focusing on the study of the Physiological Equivalent Temperature (PET) - the complete heat budget of the human body- have combined biometric and environmental sensing approaches aiming to obtain quantifiable metrics of urban thermal comfort (Piselli et al., 2018; Villadiego & Dabat, 2014). That is, it is well known that exposure to local micrometeorological stressors (e.g., direct solar radiation, high temperature, and humidity) can lead to thermal distress, which can be substantially mitigated by the implementation of UGI.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
In the systematic literature review that was conducted in the first year of thesis, using the Scopus, Web of Science and PubMed databases, A total of 2630 papers focusing on OTC assessments were retrieved and following the PRISMA 2021 guidelines and proposed exclusion criteria that disregarded studies focusing in indoor environments, those not accounting for psychophysiological dimensions, and studies focusing solely on modeling (without involving any data collection practices), were narrowed down to a total of 150 papers. To highlight the findings of the literature review, a parallel coordinate plot was created to visualize the selected 150 papers (see Figure 9). The plot includes columns for the year of publication, country where the experiment was conducted, climate type in which experiment was done, urban or park location type, the mentioned thermal indices, the mentioned environmental parameters, psychological questionnaires, physiological parameters, participant count, and the type of study which is longitudinal or cross sectional. This plot highlights trends and patterns in the existing literature, such as the commonly observed environmental parameters, or the most frequently used psychophysiological measurements in the experimental campaigns that have been carried out.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
This systematic review reveled that despite the large volume of previous research focused on OTC, only a limited amount of studies focused on physical, physiological and psychological comfort i.e. human-centered studies (L. Chen & Ng, 2012; Pigliautile & Pisello, 2018) (see Figure 1). However, it is widely recognized that an individual's internal psychological and physiological conditioning significantly influences attention and sensitivity to OTC (Nikolopoulou et al., 2001).  For example, when people from tropical climates move to temperate climates, they often adopt more clothing due to the  difference in temperature between new and native climate conditions (Jablonski & Chaplin, 2000). Furthermore, underlying medical conditions such as mental illnesses, anxiety or stress can also play a significant role in how individuals perceive thermal environments (Marques et al., 2021; Picton et al., 2020). Unusual sweating can be observed in people with mental health conditions such as depression or anxiety, due to the release of the hormone adrenaline during episodes of increased anxiety (Bahar et al., 2016). Multisensory interactions can also influence OTC. For example, excessive noise levels can influence psychological well-being and moderate thermal comfort perception (H. Chen et al., 2022), whereby stress, annoyance, and distraction caused by the noise in combination with heat, intensifies their perception of discomfort (Sepehri et al., 2019). Or a visually appealing view, such as a natural or green landscape can also positively influence thermal perception by creating a sense of psychological comfort, facilitating a perception that the environment is cooler and more comfortable (Chinazzo et al., 2021), even if the actual temperature remains the same. That is, the OTC environmental and psychophysiological benefits provided by urban design and UGI characteristics are complex and multidimensional. 
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The systematic review revealed various research gaps: 1) Prior studies generally don't account for psychophysiological thermal comfort; 2) The application of advanced biometric sensors, for neurological comfort assessments remains scarcely explored within the context of OTC assessments (Mavros et al., 2022; Zuo et al., 2021). 3) Additionally, the review identified a consistent oversight in the inclusion of Mean Radiant Temperature (MRT) and Sky View Factor (SVF) in field studies, despite their recognized impact on OTC (Koch, 1962); 4) The literature also points to a prevalence of research focusing on stationary participants, which fails to capture the dynamic nature of OTC in urban life where individuals are constantly on the move (Ueberham & Schlink, 2018; Zhang et al., 2019). 5) Lastly, the exploration of OTC during physical activities and the use of soft mobility modes such as bicycling, and walking is notably limited. Given the unique thermal stress experienced during such activities, there is a compelling need for focused geospatial and field studies that evaluate the OTC of individuals engaging in physical activities within urban settings (Lin et al., 2022). This thesis will aim at addressing these research gaps and particularly so research gap 1 and 5, to deepen our understanding of psychophysiological thermal comfort, to inform the development of more livable, inclusive, and sustainable urban environments that promote soft mobility means. The systematic review was completed in Fall 2023 and submitted to the journal Science of Total Environment. 

 Research Project 
The first working hypothesis of this research is that urban fabric characteristics, and small to medium scale UGI that is integrated within the urban fabric, modulate environmental and psychophysiological OTC. It will be the objective of this research to identify the role of urban fabric design and UGI characteristics (e.g., the ratio, geometry, type of trees, bushes, green patches, forest biodiversity) play in the enhancement of OTC specially for soft mobility users with particular focus on bicycle riders. Designing cycling routes to promote thermal comfort, emotional wellness, and reduce physiological stress levels is a challenging task given that soft mobility user physiological and psychological reactions are triggered by numerous environmental factors. 
The second working hypothesis of this research is that for the study of thermal comfort and stress, it is necessary to adopt a multi-domain (including visual, thermal and acoustic parameters) and user-centered research methodology. This research will not only include quantitative metrics, but also subjective measures to account for the participant's perception of environmental, physiological and psychological dimensions of OTC. 

To our knowledge, no prior geospatial studies have been conducted where cycling paths have been evaluated for environmental and psychophysiological OTC, nor experimental campaigns performed including cyclists to assess psychophysiological OTC and its relationship with the presence of UGI. Particularly no studies have been developed where i) microclimate sensing, ii) high spatial resolution UGI documentation, iii) physiological measurements, and iii) subjective participant assessment, have been combined, and focused on cyclists. 

 Work plan
The thesis will be organized in 4 main stages that will be developed during the 4 years of the proposed research. The research will adopt the BCR as the case study.
WP1) Systematic Review (6 months) -completed:
A systematic review titled "Environmental and psychophysiological data collection for urban outdoor thermal comfort assessment" has been developed to identify the recommended experimental practices to perform human-centered OTC field studies. This review aimed at addressing the research gaps, and identifying the micrometeorological instruments, psychological questionnaires, as well as physiological parameters which have been previously used and validated in the studied literature for human-centered OTC studies. This work high lightened research gaps including the absence of guidelines addressing human-centered OTC field experiment planning and data collection practices. To fill this gap, aside from the systematic review, this WP1 included the definition of a framework for OTC experiment planning. Journal paper #1 submitted and currently under review. As part of WP1 an application for the ethics agreement for the OTC experimental campaign (described in WP5) using environmental and psychophysiological data collection methods with participants of age 18 to 60 years has been submitted.
WP2) Geospatial documentation and UGI analysis (12 months) -in progress:
Remote sensing data to compute the Land Surface Temperature (LST) and the Normalized Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI), and NASA's Landsat (at 30-meter resolution) and Moderate Resolution Imaging Spectroradiometer (MODIS) series (at 250-meter resolution) has been compiled. From UrBIS, the LiDAR aerial point cloud and geospatial data were compiled to quantify the UGI and urban design characteristics in the bicycle routes and streets with dedicated bicycle routes in the BCR. 
On the other hand, to inform the geospatial OTC model, with the help of VITO (https://cds.climate.copernicus.eu/) data from their Copernicus Climate Change Service (C3S) containing climate variables such as air temperature, relative humidity, specific humidity and wind speed and for cities in Europe including the BCR from 2008 to 2017 have been obtained at a spatial resolution of 100 meters. 
Crossing these datasets, the OTC indices Universal Thermal Climate Index (UTCI) and Physiological Equivalent Temperature (PET) are being computed and compared for BCR biking routes. 3 conference abstracts have been submitted for presentations and a second journal paper is currently under preparation. 
WP3) Experimental campaign  -  Bicycling count and speed data (12 months)  -  in progress:
In this study, the behavior of urban cyclists in seeking thermally comfortable cycling days and times of the day is assessed. Bicycling routes have been mapped for the entire BCR region.  Google Street View (GSV) API was used to gather images which were then stitched together to create a panorama at each bicycle counter location where devices provided by Brussel Mobility measure the number of Bicycle passing through and their speed at 15-minute interval. These counters collect data for bicycle count and their average speed. Furthermore, for the same locations, using semantic deep learning approach for classifying green and non-green pixels using advanced Convolutional Neural Net (CNN) model such as Xception using PixelLib library and implemented with the DeepLabv3+ framework in Python (Llaguno-Munitxa et al., 2023), the GVI is being currently computed. Historical bike count data for the dates 1st January 2019 to 31st December 2023, has been downloaded at a 15-minute temporal resolution, while weather data for the same period has been obtained from Brussel Mobility. Using machine learning techniques, and particularly random forest algorithms, prediction models on bike behavior based on time/day of the week, prevailing weather conditions, and contextual urban design and GVI are currently being prepared and discussed to study once the UGI computations are completed. This work will form the 3[rd] journal paper of this research, and its abstract has been submitted for presentation to European General Assembly (EGU) 2024 which will take place in Vienna, Austria & Online on 14 to 19 April 2024.

WP4) Experimental campaigns (12 months)  -  planning will begin in summer 2024:
The last work of this thesis will involve the preparation of an experimental campaign. The preparation for this campaign will be held during summer 2024 and fall 2025. Additionally, biometric watches will be employed for Heart Rate Variability (HRV), Skin Conductance (SC), and Skin Temperature (ST) sensing. The selection of standardized psychological questionnaires for the experimental campaign is currently under discussion. This work will form the 4[th] journal paper of this research.

WP5 Thesis packaging and submission (6 months):
The last 6 months of the research will be dedicated to thesis writing and submission. 
